---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const roles = [
  {
    role: 'Marketing director',
    items: [
      'Campaign performance brief, assembled weekly',
      'Content request intake and triage',
      'Campaign launch coordination and checklists',
      'Context-aware email drafts for stakeholders',
      'Missing asset and pending approval alerts',
      'Weekly operational report',
    ],
  },
  {
    role: 'Real-estate professional',
    items: [
      'Lead context prepared before you call',
      'Follow-up drafts waiting for your approval',
      'Appointment preparation summaries',
      'Alerts on conversations that have gone quiet',
      'Daily priority summary',
    ],
  },
  {
    role: 'Administrator or VA',
    items: [
      'Inbox triage and routing',
      'Request intake with the details already collected',
      'Document collection chasing',
      'Recurring reminders that run themselves',
      'Status reporting',
      'Exception alerts when something is off',
    ],
  },
  {
    role: 'Small-business operator',
    items: [
      'Proposals and standard procedures drafted from your templates',
      'Customer messages prepared, never sent automatically',
      'Meeting summaries and next actions',
      'Recurring operational documents',
      'Research comparisons on demand',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Services"
  description="Two ways to work with OriginsLynk: a personal AI assistant configured around your role, or managed automation running on isolated infrastructure. Example systems by role."
>
  <section>
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Services</p>
      <h1>Two services, one ladder.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-5)">
        The difference is not how capable the system is. It is how much runs without
        you in the room — and that should follow your comfort and your evidence, not
        our price list.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PERSONAL AI ASSISTANT ============ -->
  <section class="section-sunken">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="service-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Service one · start here</p>
          <h2>Personal AI Assistant</h2>
          <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
            A capable AI assistant, installed on your own machine, configured around your
            actual role — your responsibilities, your terminology, your recurring work,
            and the boundaries you set.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>What you receive</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Installation and configuration of a supported terminal-based assistant, authenticated with your own account and subscription</li>
            <li>A role profile built from your responsibilities, tools, and approval boundaries</li>
            <li>A custom instruction system defining how it works, what it may access, and what needs review</li>
            <li>An organized workspace for documents, templates, references, and recurring tasks</li>
            <li>Reusable task templates for your most common work</li>
            <li>Two to four practical workflows, configured and tested on your real examples</li>
            <li>Safety configuration: review rules, file protections, and recovery safeguards</li>
            <li>Live training using your own work, plus a written guide and quick-reference sheet</li>
            <li>Support through the included stabilization period</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Who it suits</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            People who are interested in AI but cautious about security, complexity, and
            handing control to automated systems — and people who already pay for an AI
            subscription but only ever use the chat box.
          </p>
          <h3 style="margin-top: var(--space-6)">What it deliberately is not</h3>
          <ul class="crosslist">
            <li>Nothing runs unattended</li>
            <li>No server, no hosting, no infrastructure to maintain</li>
            <li>No broad API access granted to anyone</li>
            <li>No sending, publishing, purchasing, or deleting on your behalf</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-5)">
            You stay responsible for reviewing output before it is sent, published, or used
            to change a business record. That is the design, not a limitation of it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MANAGED AUTOMATION ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="service-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Service two · when the work is proven</p>
          <h2>Managed Automation</h2>
          <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
            The recurring work moved onto infrastructure we run, monitor, and back up, so
            it happens on schedule whether or not your laptop is open.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3>What you receive</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Workflows running on a schedule, unattended, on infrastructure isolated to you alone</li>
            <li>Connections to your email, calendar, documents, forms, and business tools</li>
            <li>Deterministic rules controlling what each workflow is permitted to do</li>
            <li>Approval points built in, so output waits for a person</li>
            <li>Monitoring with alerts that reach us before they reach you</li>
            <li>Automated daily backups, with a restore procedure that has been tested</li>
            <li>Failure investigation and minor changes included in the monthly plan</li>
            <li>Export of your workflows and data whenever you ask</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>The honest trade</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Because this runs without you, credentials you authorize are held on
            infrastructure we administer. That is a real change from the personal
            assistant, and it is the reason the two are separate services rather than
            two sizes of one.
          </p>
          <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
            What we do about it: one isolated deployment per client — separate database,
            separate credentials, separate encryption key, separate backups. Nothing is
            shared between clients. You can revoke our access from your own accounts at
            any time, without asking us.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-top: var(--space-5)">
            <a href="/trust">How access, isolation, and revocation actually work →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ EXAMPLE ROLE SYSTEMS ============ -->
  <section class="section-sunken">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Example role systems</p>
        <h2>What this looks like for a specific job.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Illustrative, not a menu. What gets built comes out of discovery — your week,
          not a template.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        {roles.map((r) => (
          <article class="card card-accent">
            <h3>{r.role}</h3>
            <ul class="card-list">
              {r.items.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ UPGRADE PATH ============ -->
  <section>
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">The path between them</p>
      <h2>Automate the thing you got tired of starting yourself.</h2>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
        The personal assistant tends to reveal which tasks you still have to remember to
        begin. Those are the honest candidates for automation — chosen from observed
        friction, not from a sales conversation.
      </p>

      <ol class="path">
        <li><strong>Personal AI Assistant.</strong> You learn to delegate safely and see where the time actually goes.</li>
        <li><strong>Assisted expansion.</strong> More templates, structured intake, a command library for your role.</li>
        <li><strong>Managed Role Automation.</strong> Scheduled workflows, approvals, monitoring, backups, isolated infrastructure.</li>
        <li><strong>Advanced role system.</strong> Cross-tool reporting, exception detection, ongoing optimization.</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="small muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-6)">
        There is no requirement to move up this list, and no discount for doing it faster.
        Plenty of people should stop at step one.
      </p>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn-primary">See pricing</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-secondary">Book a consultation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .service-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); max-width: 62ch; }

  .two-col {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-7);
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  @media (min-width: 52em) {
    .two-col { grid-template-columns: 1.25fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-8); }
  }

  .two-col h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .path {
    counter-reset: path;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .path li {
    counter-increment: path;
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--space-7);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-5);
    border-left: var(--border-thin) solid var(--rule-strong);
    margin-left: 11px;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .path li:last-child { border-left-color: transparent; padding-bottom: 0; }

  .path li::before {
    content: counter(path);
    position: absolute;
    left: -11px;
    top: 0;
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--ground);
    border: var(--border-thin) solid var(--rule-strong);
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 650;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
  }

  .path strong { color: var(--ink); }
</style>
